H: For identical applications, male teachers consistently get lower rating than female teachers
T: Hiring bias
C: UK
D: 2025
O: Economics Letter
B: Joshua Fullard, Assistant Professor of Behavioural Science at Warwick Business School carried out an experiment where he asked 615 teachers across England to evaluate fictitious job applications for three school roles: teaching assistant, classroom teacher and headteacher.  Three candidates were created for each role and their gender determined at random. 

Teachers consistently rated male applicants lower than identical female applicants. On average, male candidates received scores about one point lower on a 100-point scale.

Joshua says, “The difference may seem small but it is significant, especially when it could be a critical element in a competitive hiring process.  It could make the difference between someone being shortlisted or rejected.  Even if only a fraction of the hiring decisions made at England’s 24,000 schools each year reflect the same bias, potentially thousands of excellent teachers could be overlooked because of their gender.”
U: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0165176525001740?dgcid=SSRN_redirect_SD&ssrnid=5041245
